To find the slope of the given line $3x-\sqrt{3}y+1=0$, we first write the equation in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b$. Rearranging the given equation:
\begin{align*}
3x - \sqrt{3}y + 1 &= 0 \\
-\sqrt{3}y &= -3x - 1 \\
y &= \left(\frac{3}{\sqrt{3}}\right)x + \frac{-1}{\sqrt{3}}
\end{align*}
From this, we can see that the slope $m = \frac{3}{\sqrt{3}}$. Simplifying the slope:
\begin{align*}
m &= \frac{3}{\sqrt{3}} \\
  &= \sqrt{3}
\end{align*}
Given that the slope $m$ represents $\tan \alpha$ where $\alpha$ is the angle of inclination, we have:
\begin{align*}
\tan \alpha &= \sqrt{3}
\end{align*}
The value of $\tan \alpha = \sqrt{3}$ corresponds to an angle $\alpha = 60^\circ$. Therefore, the angle of inclination of the line is $60^\circ$.

Thus, the correct choice is $\boxed{\text{B: } 60^{\circ}}$.

