Given the function $$f(x)= \frac {2}{x}-x^{m}$$, and $$f(4)=- \frac {7}{2}$$,  
(1) Find the value of $m$;  
(2) Determine the monotonicity of $f(x)$ on $(0, +\infty)$ and provide a proof.

s:
Solution:  
(1) $$f(4)= \frac {2}{4}-4^{m}=- \frac {7}{2}$$;  
Therefore, $4^{m}=4$;  
Therefore, $m=1$;  
(2) $f(x)$ is monotonically decreasing on $(0, +\infty)$.  
Reason: $$f(x)= \frac {2}{x}-x$$, let $x_{1}>x_{2}>0$, then:  
$$f(x_{1})-f(x_{2})= \frac {2}{x_{1}}-x_{1}- \frac {2}{x_{2}}+x_{2}$$  
= $$\frac {2(x_{2}-x_{1})}{x_{1}x_{2}}+(x_{2}-x_{1})$$  
= $$(x_{2}-x_{1})( \frac {2}{x_{1}x_{2}}+1)$$;  
Since $x_{1}>x_{2}>0$;  
Therefore, $x_{2}-x_{1}<0$, and $$\frac {2}{x_{1}x_{2}}+1>0$$;  
Therefore, $f(x_{1})<f(x_{2})$;  
Therefore, $f(x)$ is monotonically decreasing on $(0, +\infty)$.

The final answers are:  
(1) $m=\boxed{1}$  
(2) $f(x)$ is monotonically decreasing on $(0, +\infty)$.

